# Mail Engine — MateMail SMTP policy integration.
#
# INSTALLED AS  /opt/mailcow-dockerized/data/conf/postfix/extra.cf
# VERIFIED AGAINST  mailcow 2026-07b (02552ffefdf0869f988edf4a7e03822e8b467b34)
#
# ── Why this file, and not an edit to main.cf ────────────────────────────────
#
# main.cf carries the banner "Please create a file extra.cf for persistent
# overrides to main.cf". mailcow's postfix.sh appends this file to the end of
# the generated main.cf on every container start, and Postfix takes the LAST
# definition of a parameter. So redefining a parameter here overrides the
# generated value without editing a file that is regenerated on every upgrade.
#
# Editing main.cf directly would work until the next `update.sh`, which rewrites
# it and would silently drop the policy hook — turning enforcement off at the
# worst possible moment, with no error anywhere.
#
# ── What was here before ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   myhostname = mx.matemail.online
#
# That line is preserved verbatim below. It is the engine's public HELO name and
# its PTR record depends on it; losing it breaks outbound authentication.

myhostname = mx.matemail.online

# The SMTP greeting customers and remote servers see. Postfix's default banner
# names the software; MateMail must not advertise its Mail Engine (DEC-011), so
# this states the hostname and the protocol and nothing else.
#
# `scripts/apply-mailcow-config.sh` appends the same line if it is missing, so
# the two agree and running it after installing this file changes nothing.
smtpd_banner = mx.matemail.online ESMTP


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MateMail policy integration (P5)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The policy service is the MateMail policy bridge sidecar, at a pinned address
# on the engine's own network. It is not published to the host and no other
# Docker network can reach it. See deploy/engine/docker-compose.override.yml.
#
# MateMail's decisions and the engine's own restrictions are deliberately BOTH
# in force. Nothing upstream is removed or relaxed below; the hook is inserted
# into the existing chain and the rest of it is reproduced exactly.


# ── Recipient restrictions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Upstream (mailcow 2026-07b) generates:
#
#   smtpd_recipient_restrictions =
#       check_recipient_mx_access proxy:mysql:.../mysql_mbr_access_maps.cf,
#       permit_sasl_authenticated,
#       permit_mynetworks,
#       check_recipient_access proxy:mysql:.../mysql_tls_enforce_in_policy.cf,
#       reject_invalid_helo_hostname,
#       reject_unauth_destination
#
# Two changes, and nothing else:
#
#   1. `permit_mynetworks` and `permit_sasl_authenticated` swap places. Both are
#      permits, and swapping two adjacent permits cannot change which clients
#      are permitted — whichever matches first permits, and a client matching
#      neither continues down the chain either way. The swap exists only to give
#      the hook a position where mynetworks has already been settled.
#
#   2. The policy hook is inserted after `permit_mynetworks` and before
#      `permit_sasl_authenticated`.
#
# WHY THE HOOK MUST PRECEDE permit_sasl_authenticated
#   This is the whole of "blocker 2". Appending the hook to the end of this
#   chain — which is what the bridge's own documentation used to advise — puts
#   it after `permit_sasl_authenticated`, so Postfix stops evaluating the moment
#   a client authenticates. Every authenticated submission, which is precisely
#   the traffic MateMail needs to rule on, would short-circuit before reaching
#   the policy service. The hook would be installed, running, and never asked.
#
# WHY THE HOOK MUST FOLLOW permit_mynetworks
#   The engine injects its own unauthenticated mail from inside the network —
#   watchdog probes and quarantine digests. MateMail knows nothing about those
#   recipients and would answer "domain not hosted here", breaking the engine's
#   own monitoring. Letting mynetworks settle first keeps engine-internal mail
#   out of customer policy entirely.
#
#   Authenticated submission from MateMail's own platform sender also arrives
#   from mynetworks (via the gateway at 10.244.0.247), so it is permitted here
#   without consulting MateMail. That is intentional and it is not a gap: the
#   end-of-data hook below has no permit in front of it, so the platform sender
#   is still fully checked and still rate limited — just once per message
#   instead of once per recipient.
#
# The three upstream entries after the hook — the TLS-enforcement lookup,
# `reject_invalid_helo_hostname` and `reject_unauth_destination` — are kept in
# their original order. The last of those is the anti-relay check, and it is the
# reason the bridge answers DUNNO rather than OK: OK would skip it.

smtpd_recipient_restrictions =
    check_recipient_mx_access proxy:mysql:/opt/postfix/conf/sql/mysql_mbr_access_maps.cf,
    permit_mynetworks,
    check_policy_service inet:10.244.0.246:10031,
    permit_sasl_authenticated,
    check_recipient_access proxy:mysql:/opt/postfix/conf/sql/mysql_tls_enforce_in_policy.cf,
    reject_invalid_helo_hostname,
    reject_unauth_destination


# ── End-of-data restrictions ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Upstream leaves `smtpd_end_of_data_restrictions` empty — verified with
# `postconf` against the running engine — so nothing is displaced here.
#
# WHY A SECOND HOOK
#   Rate limits are per MESSAGE. The recipient hook above fires once per RCPT
#   TO, so counting there would charge a sender five messages for one message
#   addressed to five people. This stage fires exactly once per message, which
#   makes it the only correct place for the counter.
#
#   The division of labour is explicit in the request MateMail receives:
#   `stage=rcpt` authorizes without counting, `stage=end_of_data` authorizes and
#   counts. The recipient hook is an early refusal, before a body is
#   transferred; this hook is the authoritative one.
#
# WHY IT IS SAFE FOR EVERYTHING ELSE
#   This restriction list contains only the hook, so there is no permit in front
#   of it and every message reaches MateMail — including the platform sender's,
#   which the recipient hook lets past via mynetworks. Unauthenticated mail is
#   answered DUNNO by the bridge at this stage, because inbound was already
#   decided at RCPT and the engine's internal injections must not be dragged
#   into customer policy.

smtpd_end_of_data_restrictions =
    check_policy_service inet:10.244.0.246:10031


# ── Deliberately NOT changed ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# smtpd_sender_restrictions
#   Upstream begins it with `reject_authenticated_sender_login_mismatch`, backed
#   by `smtpd_sender_login_maps`. That is the engine's own enforcement of
#   sender == authenticated identity, and it rejects at MAIL FROM — earlier than
#   any policy service can. MateMail enforces the same rule independently, so
#   the two agree and either alone would hold. Replacing this chain with a bare
#   policy hook — which the bridge's own documentation used to advise — would
#   have discarded that check along with `reject_unlisted_sender` and
#   `reject_unknown_sender_domain`, trading a working defence for a duplicate.
#
# smtpd_relay_restrictions
#   `permit_mynetworks, permit_sasl_authenticated, defer_unauth_destination` is
#   what makes unauthenticated relay impossible, and Postfix evaluates it
#   independently of the recipient restrictions above. It holds regardless of
#   what the policy service answers, and it must keep holding if the policy
#   service is down.
#
# smtpd_client_restrictions on the submission services
#   `permit_mynetworks, permit_sasl_authenticated, reject` in master.cf already
#   refuses an unauthenticated client on 587 outright.
#
# smtpd_milters / Rspamd, postscreen, DNS blocklists, TLS policy
#   Untouched. MateMail rules on identity and product policy; the engine keeps
#   ruling on content, reputation and transport.
#
#
# ── On upgrading mailcow ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This file reproduces part of an upstream-generated chain, so an upgrade that
# changes `smtpd_recipient_restrictions` upstream would leave this override
# silently pinned to the old list — keeping the hook, but also keeping whatever
# upstream had decided to change.
#
# `backend/tests/test_engine_policy_integration.py` compares this file against
# the upstream chain recorded there and fails if they diverge, so the
# divergence surfaces in CI rather than in production. After any mailcow
# upgrade: re-read the generated main.cf, update the expected upstream chain in
# that test, and re-derive this file from it.
